{{Economy of Balisca}}
The '''Pirihueico Canal''' ({{lang-bl|Canal de Pirihueico|link=no}}) is an artificial {{convert |82|km|adj=on|abbr=off}} waterway in [[Cerdanya]] that connects the [[W:Argentine Sea|Baliscan (Argentine) Sea]] with the South [[W:Atlantic Ocean|Atlantic Ocean]], cutting across the [[Máfil|Isthmus of Máfil]], and is a [[W:Channel (geography)|conduit]] for South Atlantic maritime trade.

The Pirihueico Canal shortcut greatly reduces the time for ships to travel between the [[W:Southern Cone|Southern Cone]] and the South Atlantic, enabling them to avoid the lengthy and hazardous routes through the [[Strait of Ivorra]] and the archipelago of [[Cap de Pingüins]] in the [[W:Drake Passage|Drake Passage]]. This southern maritime region is commonly referred to as the [[Graveyard of the South Atlantic]]. 

